WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.767 --> 00:00:03.767
-I'm talking to the one and only
Jason Bateman right now.

00:00:03.767 --> 00:00:04.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:04.767 --> 00:00:08.133
That's exactly right.

00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:09.734
I want to talk "Ozark."

00:00:09.734 --> 00:00:13.100
"Ozark" is a very spoilery show,
and so I don't want to spoil

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:14.501
anything for anyone
who hasn't seen it.

00:00:14.501 --> 00:00:16.167
-We all die at the end.
Is that a spoiler?

00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:17.801
-You can't even -- you can't
even joke and say that.

00:00:17.801 --> 00:00:20.167
-But it's good to -- Yeah.
-You can't even joke.

00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:22.934
-It's an old coyote --
You know, the dynamite thing?

00:00:22.934 --> 00:00:25.033
-No, that's not exactly
what happened. [ Laughs ]

00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:27.868
-It's fine.
-That would be unbelievable.

00:00:27.868 --> 00:00:29.901
Who -- Do people try
to get spoilers out of you?

00:00:29.901 --> 00:00:33.000
Because I wouldn't.
I would not try to do that.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.701
-No. My wife kind of.

00:00:36.701 --> 00:00:38.968
And then I'll start to --
-She must know.

00:00:38.968 --> 00:00:44.601
-She doesn't really know
much of anything.

00:00:44.601 --> 00:00:46.701
I -- I made such a mistake,
Jimmy.

00:00:46.701 --> 00:00:47.734
No, no.
-Oh, my God.

00:00:47.734 --> 00:00:48.968
You're going to be
in big trouble.

00:00:48.968 --> 00:00:51.267
-Oh, she knows I'm joking.
-She's the greatest.

00:00:51.267 --> 00:00:53.000
-She does try to --
-Oh, because you know why?

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.434
Because then
she'll be in trouble,

00:00:54.434 --> 00:00:56.334
because if she'll go to a dinner
or something,

00:00:56.334 --> 00:00:58.834
then she has to lie and go,
"I can't tell you."

00:00:58.834 --> 00:00:59.834
-Right, right.
-So don't put her

00:00:59.834 --> 00:01:01.100
in that position.
-Exactly.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.501
-That kind of -- Does that help?

00:01:03.501 --> 00:01:07.300
-I -- No one really tries

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:13.133
to get the spoilers out,
but I do like to just go ahead

00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:14.868
and offer them,
like I just did here.

00:01:14.868 --> 00:01:17.100
I mean, but the death
the four of us get --

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.133
-No, no, no, no, no.
You can't.

00:01:19.133 --> 00:01:22.033
But have you accidentally
spoiled anything for anyone?

00:01:22.033 --> 00:01:27.133
-Yeah, I was actually --
I was actually watching

00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:28.834
a cut of one of these scenes

00:01:28.834 --> 00:01:30.868
that we was working
with the editor,

00:01:30.868 --> 00:01:32.801
and I was watching it
on my computer,

00:01:32.801 --> 00:01:36.667
and Amanda, my wife,
walked in.

00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:39.667
And she walked in,
she was talking to me.

00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:42.901
"Hey, so. blah, blah, blah --"
And she looks and she watched

00:01:42.901 --> 00:01:45.033
one of the characters die,

00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:47.133
and she was like
"God, d--"

00:01:47.133 --> 00:01:48.701
[ Laughter ]

00:01:48.701 --> 00:01:52.100
-Like, "Why did I walk in now?
And now it's ruined."

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.367
-Yeah.
-Cause that's one of the --

00:01:53.367 --> 00:01:55.000
the twists that come in,
I remember --

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.701
Questlove, you saw "Ozark" in,
like, a day or so?

00:01:58.701 --> 00:02:00.067
-Yes.
-And so --

00:02:00.067 --> 00:02:01.934
-Come on.
Quest was like, "Dude,

00:02:01.934 --> 00:02:04.100
where are you at?"
I'm like, "It's so interesting

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:05.100
and so fun."

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.467
And he's like,
"No, no, no.

00:02:06.467 --> 00:02:08.601
Have you got to anything crazy?"
I go, "Nothing crazy yet."

00:02:08.601 --> 00:02:10.734
-Didn't "SNL" do
a sketch on that,

00:02:10.734 --> 00:02:12.234
where like with streaming shows,

00:02:12.234 --> 00:02:14.968
you can't talk about any show.

00:02:14.968 --> 00:02:16.868
"Are you watching it?"
"Don't talk about that."

00:02:16.868 --> 00:02:18.634
And then they talk about it,
and don't talk about that.

00:02:18.634 --> 00:02:20.100
-No, you're not allowed
to talk about anything.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:21.734
-One of the bummers
about streaming,

00:02:21.734 --> 00:02:24.167
but I like having the choice

00:02:24.167 --> 00:02:26.000
of being able
to watch them all at once.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.133
-How long has it been and when
did you start doing "Ozark?"

00:02:28.133 --> 00:02:32.133
Five -- And so, this is
the fourth -- five years.

00:02:32.133 --> 00:02:34.334
-Five years.
-Yeah, but maybe six with COVID.

00:02:34.334 --> 00:02:35.767
-Yeah.
-I don't know.

00:02:35.767 --> 00:02:38.467
-So six years. I think you
should catch up and understand.

00:02:38.467 --> 00:02:40.067
I think it's totally
understandable

00:02:40.067 --> 00:02:42.067
if someone does spoil it.
I won't do it,

00:02:42.067 --> 00:02:45.200
but I will tell you that
it's one of the greatest shows.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:48.868
Every performance is knockout.
Laura Linney is a knockout.

00:02:48.868 --> 00:02:50.634
A knockout.

00:02:50.634 --> 00:02:51.868
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:51.868 --> 00:02:53.634
Julia, a knockout.

00:02:53.634 --> 00:02:55.200
You are -- dude,
you're unbelievable.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:58.534
I can't imagine any other
human being doing you.

00:02:58.534 --> 00:03:00.467
I tried to do
an impression of you,

00:03:00.467 --> 00:03:02.133
and now -- I know.

00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:03.634
-Didn't you do one
last time I was here?

00:03:03.634 --> 00:03:05.400
-I did, and now people ask me
to do it.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.601
They go, "Can you --"
-Wait, I kind of forget.

00:03:07.601 --> 00:03:09.667
It's hurtful, I think,
probably, right?

00:03:09.667 --> 00:03:10.868
[ Laughter ]

00:03:10.868 --> 00:03:12.701
-Most impressions are,
but no, it's out of love.

00:03:12.701 --> 00:03:14.300
-All you needed to do
is put on slippers,

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:18.033
PJs, and watch a Dodger game
and fall asleep. That's perfect.

00:03:18.033 --> 00:03:19.267
-I was just more like,

00:03:19.267 --> 00:03:21.067
[imitating Jason] "G-Gang? Gang?
Gang? Guys? Guys?"

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:23.400
-[ Laughs ]
-"Guys?"

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:24.767
[Normally] That's all
I really did.

00:03:24.767 --> 00:03:26.133
That's all.
-That's pretty good.

00:03:26.133 --> 00:03:28.801
-[Imitating Jason]"G-Guy--
G-Guy-- Guys? OK, guys? OK?"

00:03:28.801 --> 00:03:30.734
[Normally]
But I can't -- you know.

00:03:30.734 --> 00:03:35.334
I got to -- I got to say,
I also did some deep diving,

00:03:35.334 --> 00:03:37.133
and I might be wrong
in saying this,

00:03:37.133 --> 00:03:39.434
but do you come up
with the drawings?

00:03:39.434 --> 00:03:41.334
The like -- kind of
the graphics?

00:03:41.334 --> 00:03:42.901
-The little symbols
at the beginning?

00:03:42.901 --> 00:03:44.167
-Yes.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:44.167 --> 00:03:45.667
-Is that you?
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:45.667 --> 00:03:48.300
I work with this great graphic
artist, Neil Kellerhouse,

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.267
who's worked
a lot with David Fincher,

00:03:50.267 --> 00:03:51.968
and I'm a huge Fincher fan.

00:03:51.968 --> 00:03:54.801
And so I had reached out to him
and I said, "So here's the deal.

00:03:54.801 --> 00:03:57.968
We got to do Netflix --
we got to do an open --

00:03:57.968 --> 00:04:00.133
I didn't want to do,
like, a credits sequence.

00:04:00.133 --> 00:04:02.367
Like, don't you hate
you got to watch a minute

00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:04.634
and a half of credits for a show
you watch all the time.

00:04:04.634 --> 00:04:05.901
It's like, let's get
to the story.

00:04:05.901 --> 00:04:07.067
So I wanted to do something
that was just, like,

00:04:07.067 --> 00:04:09.033
5 minutes -- 5 seconds long.

00:04:09.033 --> 00:04:12.634
But Netflix was like, "We got to
see the name of the show."

00:04:12.634 --> 00:04:14.334
Like, people have already
clicked.

00:04:14.334 --> 00:04:15.634
They know what show
they're watching.

00:04:15.634 --> 00:04:17.767
-[ Laughs ] Yeah, like, "What?
Is this 'Bozark?'

00:04:17.767 --> 00:04:19.334
We never heard of that."
You go, "No, no."

00:04:19.334 --> 00:04:20.934
We know what we're watching.

00:04:20.934 --> 00:04:22.400
-So I said, "All right,
all right, all right.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:24.767
So we're going to put the name
of the show in this --

00:04:24.767 --> 00:04:27.634
in this little 5-second thing,
but we're going to not do it

00:04:27.634 --> 00:04:29.100
with O-Z-A-R-K,

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.767
we'll do symbols
that are in the shape of Z-R --

00:04:32.767 --> 00:04:34.467
Z-A-R-K.
Which I don't know

00:04:34.467 --> 00:04:37.100
if a lot of people know that --

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:39.601
-I didn't know that.
-The symbols are in the shape.

00:04:39.601 --> 00:04:42.734
So it's -- you got the "O,"
and then the first symbol

00:04:42.734 --> 00:04:43.968
is, if you look closely,

00:04:43.968 --> 00:04:45.501
it's a "Z" then an "A,"
then an "R."

00:04:45.501 --> 00:04:47.200
-Are you serious?
-But those symbols

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:49.667
are also harbingers of things

00:04:49.667 --> 00:04:51.000
that are coming
in the episode.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:52.234
-Right.
That's why Neil

00:04:52.234 --> 00:04:54.334
is such a genius.
He can work all this stuff out.

00:04:54.334 --> 00:04:56.200
-I watch -- I go through
every episode

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:58.801
and I give him, like, 10 or 12
things that are important

00:04:58.801 --> 00:05:00.567
in the episode,
and then he decides

00:05:00.567 --> 00:05:03.400
which one of those things
lend themselves to a symbol.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:06.267
Like I'll say,
a fish plays a big part.

00:05:06.267 --> 00:05:07.567
And so he'll go,
"Oh, yeah, okay.

00:05:07.567 --> 00:05:10.567
So a fish can bend into an 'R'
or a 'K,' or..."

00:05:10.567 --> 00:05:12.000
So he works on it,
then he sends them to me.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:13.901
I go, "Yeah, I make --
that's too obvious.

00:05:13.901 --> 00:05:16.501
Make that a little less so --
it looks too much like a 'K.'"

00:05:16.501 --> 00:05:19.968
So that's a fun thing we do
together.

00:05:19.968 --> 00:05:21.634
It's brilliant
because you're like,

00:05:21.634 --> 00:05:23.834
"Wait --" You try to almost
guess what the episode

00:05:23.834 --> 00:05:25.234
would be about. Why?

00:05:25.234 --> 00:05:26.601
I'm not even -- I'm never close.

00:05:26.601 --> 00:05:29.200
-The only rule is that it can't
be something that existed

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:30.701
before you go to that card.

00:05:30.701 --> 00:05:32.133
In other words,
that little cold opening,

00:05:32.133 --> 00:05:33.367
it can't be
anything from there.

00:05:33.367 --> 00:05:34.367
-No.
-So it's from that point

00:05:34.367 --> 00:05:35.601
forward.

00:05:35.601 --> 00:05:38.501
-And so you're doing this,
the series finale.

00:05:38.501 --> 00:05:41.267
You do -- I don't know what it
is, but your last scene

00:05:41.267 --> 00:05:43.601
with Laura Linney.
I don't know what it is,

00:05:43.601 --> 00:05:47.400
but when you do that,
there are tears.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:50.834
-There some --
the actual -- the last scene

00:05:50.834 --> 00:05:54.000
we shot of everything was a --

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.334
was a big moment in the show.

00:05:57.334 --> 00:05:58.601
It's not the last scene
in the show,

00:05:58.601 --> 00:06:00.701
but it's the last scene
that we shot.

00:06:00.701 --> 00:06:02.534
So, yeah, there was
some melancholy.

00:06:02.534 --> 00:06:04.300
It was kind of -- but you know,
it's bittersweet

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:07.267
because we were all really happy
that we kind of made it

00:06:07.267 --> 00:06:10.033
to the finish line
without screwing things up

00:06:10.033 --> 00:06:11.968
too bad, I hope.
-You made -- No.

00:06:11.968 --> 00:06:13.601
You made great TV.
-Thank you.

00:06:13.601 --> 00:06:15.567
-I mean,
that's even bigger than TV.

00:06:15.567 --> 00:06:17.400
I don't know what --
it's like the movies.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.400
Like mini movies.
They're unbelievable.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:22.534
-A bunch of incredible people
in that crew

00:06:22.534 --> 00:06:23.868
and the incredible writing,

00:06:23.868 --> 00:06:27.501
and it was -- we were
very, very lucky.

00:06:27.501 --> 00:06:29.367
-Is there any world where
there's a happy ending

00:06:29.367 --> 00:06:30.734
to the show?

00:06:30.734 --> 00:06:32.934
-It is. Well, that's what
Chris Mundy,

00:06:32.934 --> 00:06:37.000
who is our incredible writer and
his staff and our showrunner,

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:41.000
he wants -- you know,
because he and I, we talk

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.200
before the beginning
of every season

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:43.667
about what are we going
to try to get done,

00:06:43.667 --> 00:06:45.901
and then I don't mess around
with the writing.

00:06:45.901 --> 00:06:48.834
I'm not smart enough.
But we talk about the themes.

00:06:48.834 --> 00:06:51.000
And with the final season,
the whole thing was like,

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:52.467
"Well, how are we
going to end it?"

00:06:52.467 --> 00:06:55.667
Should the Byrde family
pay a bill?

00:06:55.667 --> 00:06:57.300
You know? Like, should
they get away with it?

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:58.434
Should they not?"

00:06:58.434 --> 00:07:00.067
And so he said,
"Well, I want it to be

00:07:00.067 --> 00:07:02.701
a happy ending,
but there's got to be

00:07:02.701 --> 00:07:06.167
a little bit of a,
'Well, is it happy for them?'"

00:07:06.167 --> 00:07:09.200
And so hopefully,
the audience will think,

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:10.834
"Ah, they've kind of threaded
the needle

00:07:10.834 --> 00:07:13.434
between a happy ending,
but that there's a little bit

00:07:13.434 --> 00:07:15.701
of -- they're limping.
-Yeah.

00:07:15.701 --> 00:07:17.534
-Did you --
Do you know going into it

00:07:17.534 --> 00:07:20.133
where it was going to end up?
Like, from the first season?

00:07:20.133 --> 00:07:23.067
You're like, "Let's do this.
Let's hope that it's a hit."

00:07:23.067 --> 00:07:25.701
-We didn't have the finish line
done at the very,

00:07:25.701 --> 00:07:28.734
very beginning,
but we were pretty clear

00:07:28.734 --> 00:07:31.067
that it should be
a family show --

00:07:31.067 --> 00:07:34.601
not a family show --
Not an AA -0-

00:07:34.601 --> 00:07:35.601
[ Laughter ]
-It is so not a family show.

00:07:35.601 --> 00:07:37.467
It is far from it.

00:07:37.467 --> 00:07:38.801
[ Laughter ]

00:07:38.801 --> 00:07:41.501
-A show about a family.
-Yeah, it's a morning show.

00:07:41.501 --> 00:07:43.200
You really got to watch it
with the kids.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:44.734
You got to get the kids over.
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:44.734 --> 00:07:46.467
They love it. Oh, my God.

00:07:46.467 --> 00:07:50.567
-Yeah. A lead-in to "Alf."
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:50.567 --> 00:07:52.334
Oh, my gosh.

00:07:52.334 --> 00:07:54.234
Congrats, buddy,
on everything.

00:07:54.234 --> 00:07:55.734
You know I love you.
I want to show a clip.

00:07:55.734 --> 00:07:58.067
Here's Jason Bateman
and Laura Linney in "Ozark".

00:07:58.067 --> 00:07:59.801
Take a look at this.

00:07:59.801 --> 00:08:03.534
-You suggested this life.

00:08:03.534 --> 00:08:06.834
-Long time ago.

00:08:06.834 --> 00:08:10.968
-You knew better
than me what this was.

00:08:10.968 --> 00:08:14.167
They cut out a man's eyes
in front of you.

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:20.467
-Hey. I got us into this.

00:08:20.467 --> 00:08:22.200
You're getting us out of this.

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:26.033
Yeah, we --

00:08:26.033 --> 00:08:29.434
We chose this together.

00:08:29.434 --> 00:08:32.334
Nobody talked you into this.
You were excited about this.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:39.501
-You were a good salesman.

00:08:39.501 --> 00:08:40.567
-Oh.

00:08:40.567 --> 00:08:41.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:41.634 --> 00:08:44.000
Come on.
Jason Bateman.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:46.834
"Ozark" returns
for its final episodes

00:08:46.834 --> 00:08:49.400
April 29 on Netflix.

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:51.300
Steven, hello.

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:52.467
More "Tonight Show"
after the break.

